// Editable component-output table (v0.663) -- closes gap #4 in
// docs/design/homestead-solo-design.md section 7 and implements the "editable
// component-output table (infinite-of-X data)" that docs/design/self-sufficiency.md's
// "What data we'd add" section calls for. One entry per generation / collection / recycling
// machine in data/machines/home.ron's catalog: an output figure + its assumptions, so the
// self-sufficiency score is COMPUTED from a queryable data file instead of parsed out of the
// hand-typed stat strings baked into home.ron. Every `id` here MUST be a machine in the
// home.ron catalog (enforced by a unit test). Loaded by src/systems/self_sufficiency.rs.
// Data-driven (infinite-of-X): tune a number or add a machine here, no code change.
//
// FIELDS:
//   id           - a machine catalog id in data/machines/home.ron (and home_solo.ron).
//   output_value - the machine's per-unit output in `unit`.
//   unit         - the output's unit. NOTE the unit is HETEROGENEOUS across rows (kWh/day for
//                  energy generation, L/day for water collection, kg/day for nutrient
//                  recycling, kW for a runtime-gated backup, L/day-air for life support). The
//                  loader's household_energy_balance() sums only the "kWh/day" rows for the
//                  energy score; a water/nutrient/air score sums its own unit the same way.
//   assumptions  - the editable, human-readable derivation the operator can tune.
//
// AGREEMENT with home.ron / homestead-solo-design.md: every value below was cross-checked
// against the catalog stat strings + design math and AGREES internally (e.g. solar 400 W x
// 4.5 sun-h x 0.80 = 1.44 kWh/day, which the catalog rounds to "+1.4 kWh/d"; 8 x 1.44 + 0.7
// wind = 12.2 kWh/day, the home.ron Energy-loop supply line).
//
// DISAGREEMENT FOUND 2026-07-30 (life-support balance pass, see data/home_outline.json):
// the solar figure below agrees with the DESIGN DOC but NOT with location.ron sitting in this
// same folder. The 4.5 sun-hours is a generic "temperate" assumption with no place attached;
// location.ron names Silverdale, WA, where NREL's NSRDB gives 3.9 peak sun-hours annual
// average and about 1.3 in December. At that site the honest figures are 1.17 kWh/day annual
// and 0.39 kWh/day in December, and an off-grid array is sized on December, not the average.
// The values below are LEFT AS THEY ARE on purpose: `household_energy_supply_sums_kwh_per_day_
// components` in src/systems/self_sufficiency.rs pins 4 panels to 5.76 +/- 0.5 kWh/day, so
// correcting the number needs a code change alongside it, not a lone data edit. Do not treat
// 1.44 as location-honest until that test moves with it.
(
    components: [
        // ── Energy GENERATION (kWh/day, so household_energy_balance() sums these) ──
        (
            id: "solar_panel", output_value: 1.44, unit: "kWh/day",
            assumptions: "400 W peak x 4.5 sun-hours/day (generic temperate, NO location) x 0.80 system losses (inverter + wiring + charge). Catalog rounds to +1.4 kWh/d. CONTESTED: at location.ron's own site (Silverdale, WA) NREL gives 3.9 sun-hours annual and 1.3 in December, and 0.80 omits battery round-trip, so the honest derate is 0.75, giving 1.17 kWh/day annual and 0.39 kWh/day in December. Correcting this value fails a pinned unit test; see the file header.",
        ),
        (
            id: "wind_turbine_small", output_value: 0.7, unit: "kWh/day",
            assumptions: "150 W rated x ~19% capacity factor (low-wind inland site) ~= 0.7 kWh/day. Matches the catalog +0.7 kWh/d.",
        ),
        // ── Backup generation (rated power, NOT a daily energy -- unit differs on purpose;
        //    excluded from the daily kWh/day supply sum until runtime hours are modeled) ──
        (
            id: "generator_portable", output_value: 2.0, unit: "kW",
            assumptions: "2 kW fuel-burning backup. Rated power, not a daily figure -- daily kWh depends on run-hours, so it is NOT summed into the passive kWh/day supply. Covers the deep no-sun-no-wind tail.",
        ),
        // ── Water COLLECTION / treatment (L/day) ──
        (
            id: "water_tank", output_value: 144.0, unit: "L/day",
            assumptions: "Passive rain catchment at 0.1 L/min (home.ron port flow) = 144 L/day, no power. Also stores 8000 L (the dry-season buffer).",
        ),
        (
            id: "water_pump", output_value: 2880.0, unit: "L/day",
            assumptions: "Powered well lift at 2.0 L/min (home.ron port) = 2880 L/day WHEN RUN. Power-gated: cutting the grid stops the inflow. Runs a fraction of the day in practice.",
        ),
        (
            id: "water_purifier", output_value: 240.0, unit: "L/day",
            assumptions: "+240 L/day potable throughput (home.ron stat). Polishes water the pump lifted; not a net source, so it is a treatment cap, not new supply.",
        ),
        // ── Nutrient / waste RECYCLING (kg/day nutrient; also +greywater) ──
        (
            id: "composter", output_value: 1.2, unit: "kg/day",
            assumptions: "+1.2 kg/day of FINISHED COMPOST (not of nutrient: the actual N+P+K in 1.2 kg of compost is about 18 g N, 6 g P, 12 g K, roughly 70x smaller) from food + crop + human waste, plus greywater back to irrigation (home.ron stats). NOTE the scale: home.ron is the 3-PERSON home, so its +155 L/day greywater goes with a 240 L/day household, a plausible 65% recovery. For ONE person the household is 85 L/day and greywater is about 59 L/day; the 155 figure must never be copied into a solo design, where it would imply 194% recovery. One person makes about 4.3 kg/day of wet organic material once crop residue is counted, not 1.5 kg/day (that figure is excreta only).",
        ),
        // ── Air GENERATION / life support (L/day of scrubbed fresh air) ──
        (
            id: "air_recycler", output_value: 28800.0, unit: "L/day-air",
            assumptions: "20 L/min O2-making / CO2-scrubbing blower (home.ron Air OUT port) = 28800 L/day. Sized for a ~3-person sealed household with margin; priority-1 (shed last). CONTESTED (2026-07-30): this is an AIR THROUGHPUT figure, and the machine's 25 W draw is about an order of magnitude below what actually MAKING oxygen costs. One person at 2,600 kcal/day consumes 0.77 kg O2/day; electrolysis runs about 6.5 kWh per kg of O2, so 5.0 kWh/day, plus about 0.6 for CO2 removal and 0.2 for trace contaminants. That 5.8 kWh/day is 145% of the whole solo home's 4.0 kWh/day budget. 25 W (0.6 kWh/day) is right for a blower that MOVES air, not for one that regenerates it.",
        ),
    ],
)
